sweet ufs: keep status writes off the LUN scan, report lock owner + flush stage

The first flush (seq 1) wrote the modem status block through
sweet_ufs_pwrite(..., sweet_ufs_part("darwin", ...)): that helper loops over
eight LUNs, a LUN that never answers costs a full command timeout each, and
the whole scan runs with sweet_ufs_lock held. Result: every file I/O request
in the system blocks a few seconds after launchd starts, nothing prints (the
console is behind the same lock) and the boot looks dead while the kernel is
alive. Seen on bp11 (modem=0): heartbeats to 270 s, "beats 2", command serial
frozen - the flusher was the only stuck thread.

Write the status block with one ufs_write_blocks() into the block just below
the ring header (same partition by construction, no lookup), and report
lock_owner/lock_users/flush_stage in the heartbeat so the next unexplained
stop names the path holding the lock.

